     "text": "Called an emergency vet clinic to treat a standard ear infection on my dog.\n\nQuote came back at $940.\n\nI asked the receptionist how long the procedure takes.\n\nShe goes: \"about 10 minutes.\"\n\nI said: \"so I'm paying $5,600 an hour for labor?\"\n\nShe looked around and whispered:\n\n\"I make $18 an hour. The private equity health conglomerate that bought out our local vet clinic last year sets mandatory revenue quotas on every single exam room.\"\n\nThen she showed me her tablet.\n\nIf she doesn't hit $2,500 a day in diagnostic upsells, her clinic manager writes her up.\n\nThe original family vet ran that practice for 25 years charging $75 a visit and knew every animal by name.\n\nNow Wall Street private equity owns veterinary clinics, dental chains, and optical stores across the country….. jacked up prices by 400%, and put animal caretakers on corporate sales commissions.\n\nYou aren't paying for veterinary care anymore.\n\nYou're funding a private equity fund's debt service on a basic ear drop that costs $4 to manufacture.",
